Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: Gothic period live-action, set in the 1840s.

FADE IN:

INT. MAGISTRATE'S CHAMBER - DAY

A bare chamber with high walls and a shuttered window. A blackened-tin lantern rests upon a plain table.

THE NARRATOR, a gaunt man in his thirties with lank black hair, a sharp clean-shaven face, a black frock coat, and a gray waistcoat, sits rigidly before us.

THE NARRATOR
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

A fly scratches against the window. A carriage rattles far outside. Within the wall, tiny claws disturb the plaster. His eyes follow every sound.

THE NARRATOR
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell.

He leans forward, forcing his trembling hands flat upon the table.

THE NARRATOR
How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

His gaze settles upon the lantern.

THE NARRATOR
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none.

INT. OLD HOUSE - SITTING ROOM - EVENING

Firelight warms a close, richly furnished room. A few gold coins and keys lie untouched beside a tea tray.

THE OLD MAN, frail and deep-lined, with silver hair and one pale-blue eye filmed by a cloudy veil, reads beside the fire in a burgundy robe.

The Narrator serves him tea with solicitous care.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire.

The Old Man looks up. Firelight catches the clouded eye.

The Narrator's smile freezes. Tea trembles against the rim of the cup.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I think it was his eye! Yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale-blue eye, with a film over it.

The eye remains fixed upon him. His free hand closes until the nails bite his palm.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. MAGISTRATE'S CHAMBER - DAY

The Narrator releases the lantern's fastening with a soft metallic CLICK.

THE NARRATOR
Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation!

He smiles with private pride.

THE NARRATOR
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him. And every night, about midnight—

INT. OLD HOUSE - OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Closed shutters seal the chamber in blackness. The Old Man sleeps in a high wooden bed, his silver hair spread across the pillow.

The latch turns by a hair's breadth. The door opens upon the Narrator's eye and the blackened lantern.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I opened it—oh, so gently! When I had made an opening sufficient for my head, I put in a dark lantern, all closed, closed, that no light shone out.

His head advances through the opening almost imperceptibly. A candle gutters inside the lantern while the room remains dark.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I moved it slowly—very, very slowly, so that I might not disturb the old man's sleep. It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening.

He eases the lantern shutter. Its hinges CREAK. A thread of light reaches the Old Man's closed eyelid.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ha!—would a madman have been so wise as this?

Seven nights pass in matched images: the latch turns, the head enters, the lantern opens, and the thin ray finds the same closed eye.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
This I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed. It was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD HOUSE - SITTING ROOM - MORNING

Morning light fills the room. The Narrator enters cheerfully with the Old Man's breakfast.

THE NARRATOR
Good morning. How have you passed the night?

The Old Man smiles and clasps his arm, unsuspecting. The Narrator meets the closed, clouded eye without flinching.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. OLD HOUSE - OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

The eighth night. The Narrator's hand creeps around the door. Beside his thumb, the latch moves slower than the minute hand of a watch.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious. Never before had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity.

He stifles a chuckle.

The Old Man moves suddenly beneath the blankets. The Narrator does not retreat. He pushes the door steadily onward through the pitch-black room.

His head enters. His thumb slips upon the lantern's tin fastening.

CLICK.

The Old Man springs upright.

THE OLD MAN
Who's there?

The Narrator becomes perfectly still.

A deathwatch beetle TICKS inside the wall. The Old Man remains upright, listening. The lantern grows hot in the Narrator's clenched hand.

Distant quarter-hours sound, one after another.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and I did not hear him lie down. He was still sitting up in the bed listening—just as I have done, night after night, hearkening to the death watches in the wall.

A slight, stifled GROAN rises from the bed.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the groan of mortal terror—the low sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe. I knew the sound well.

The Old Man grips his blanket and peers into the darkness.

THE OLD MAN
It is nothing but the wind in the chimney... only a mouse crossing the floor... merely a cricket.

The Narrator's thin smile flickers with pity, then returns.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
All in vain; because Death, in approaching him, had stalked with his black shadow before him, and enveloped the victim.

After another long silence, the Narrator moves one finger. The lantern's shutter opens a very, very little.

A single dim ray, like the thread of a spider, shoots across the room and falls precisely upon the eye.

It is open—wide, wide open. Dull blue beneath its hideous veil.

The Narrator scarcely breathes. His hand holds the ray motionless while fury tightens his face.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I could see nothing else of the old man's face or person. I had directed the ray, as if by instinct, precisely upon the damned spot.

A faint, rapid THUD begins.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
What you mistake for madness is but over-acuteness of the sense. There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

The Narrator's eyes widen. The Old Man's fingers knot in the blanket.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

The THUD grows quicker. Louder.

The Narrator holds the lantern steady, but sweat runs down his face.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The hellish tattoo increased. It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant.

The eye stares into the ray. The heartbeat pounds against the silence of the old house.

The Narrator glances toward the adjoining wall.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
A new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man's hour had come!

He throws the lantern wide and leaps into the room with a yell.

The Old Man shrieks once—once only.

The Narrator drags him to the floor and pulls the heavy bed over him. The heartbeat continues beneath it, muffled but relentless.

The Narrator kneels upon the bed frame, smiling as he presses down. Dust settles around him. The lantern flame shortens.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
For many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound. This did not vex me; it would not be heard through the wall.

The THUD falters.

Silence.

The Narrator removes the bed. He kneels beside the body and holds his hand over the heart for a long while.

INT. MAGISTRATE'S CHAMBER - DAY

The Narrator presses his own palm flat upon the table.

THE NARRATOR
Yes, he was stone, stone dead. There was no pulsation. His eye would trouble me no more.

He closes the lantern.

THE NARRATOR
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

INT. OLD HOUSE - OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

The night wanes. A saw rises and falls above a deep tub. One by one, cloth-wrapped bundles accumulate in the lantern light.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I worked hastily, but in silence. First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs.

The Narrator pries exactly three planks from the chamber floor. He lowers every bundle between the scantlings.

He replaces the boards, tapping each one snugly into its old position.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I replaced them so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected anything wrong.

The floor is spotless. The tub stands nearby, its lid secured.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

A distant bell begins to strike four.

INT. OLD HOUSE - ENTRANCE HALL AND STAIRS - PRE-DAWN

The fourth stroke fades. Three firm KNOCKS sound at the street door.

The Narrator, immaculate once more in his black coat and gray waistcoat, opens it with a light heart.

OFFICER REYNOLDS, tall with sandy whiskers, stands beside broad, dark-bearded OFFICER HALE and young, clean-shaven OFFICER BELL. All three wear dark-blue period police coats.

OFFICER REYNOLDS
We are officers of the police. A shriek was heard by a neighbour during the night. We have been deputed to search the premises.

The Narrator smiles and opens the door wide.

THE NARRATOR
Gentlemen, welcome. The shriek was my own, in a dream. The old man is absent in the country. Search—search well.

He surrenders every key. The officers examine the entrance, cupboards, and locks before following him up the stairs.

INT. OLD HOUSE - OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

The Narrator throws open the chamber. The bed is orderly. The Old Man's gold, keys, and papers remain secure and undisturbed.

The officers inspect every corner. Officer Bell glances across the seamless floorboards and finds nothing.

The Narrator brings in four chairs. He places his own seat directly above the hidden body.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease.

The officers sit and chat of familiar things. The Narrator answers cheerily.

A thin RING needles through the room. His smile stiffens.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
But, ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears.

The ringing condenses into a low, rapid THUD.

The officers continue their pleasant conversation. The Narrator speaks more freely, but his words recede beneath the pulse.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It continued and gained definiteness—until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

INT. MAGISTRATE'S CHAMBER - DAY

The same THUD intrudes upon the quiet chamber. The Narrator's fingers curl against the tabletop.

THE NARRATOR
No doubt I now grew very pale;—but I talked more fluently, and with a heightened voice. Yet the sound increased—and what could I do?

The lantern's loose fastening trembles with every beat.

THE NARRATOR
I gasped for breath—and yet the officers heard it not. I talked more quickly—more vehemently; but the noise steadily increased.

INT. OLD HOUSE - OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

The Narrator rises and argues about trifles, gesticulating violently. Reynolds, Hale, and Bell remain seated.

The heartbeat grows louder.

The Narrator paces across the concealed body with heavy strides.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would they not be gone?

The officers chat pleasantly and smile.

The Narrator snatches up his chair, swings it aside, and grates its legs across the planks.

The heartbeat rises above the scrape—louder, louder, louder.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!

The officers' calm smiles distort in the Narrator's terrified gaze.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Anything was better than this agony! I could bear those hypocritical smiles no longer!

The heartbeat becomes deafening.

The Narrator wheels upon them and points to the floor.

THE NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

The officers' smiles vanish.

The heartbeat cuts to silence.

INT. MAGISTRATE'S CHAMBER - DAY

The Narrator's outstretched hand slowly lowers to the table.

His own pulse jumps visibly in his throat.

He listens.

> FADE OUT.

> THE END <
